"# Appendix U — Comparison recipes

Chapter 9 said comparison depends on kind. This appendix is the cookbook.

## Money — same currency, then amount

We represent each typed value as a tagged list and give it a plist accessor. The comparison verbs check the tag/unit first, then the magnitude — returning a Result-error when the kinds don't line up.

```scheme
(define (money . fields) (cons 'money fields))
(define (field record key)
  (let loop ((xs (cdr record)))
    (cond ((null? xs) #f)
          ((eq? (car xs) key) (cadr xs))
          (else (loop (cddr xs))))))
(define (result/error sym . ctx) (list 'result/error sym ctx))

(define (money< a b)
  (if (eq? (field a :currency) (field b :currency))
      (< (field a :amount) (field b :amount))
      (result/error 'currency-mismatch
        :ctx (list :a-currency (field a :currency)
                   :b-currency (field b :currency)
                   :hint \"call money-convert first\"))))
```

Two money values in different currencies cannot be compared directly. Comparison across currencies would require a canonical currency (USD by default) and a rate lookup — see the currency table appendix.

```scheme
(money< (money :amount 1234 :currency 'USD)
        (money :amount 1500 :currency 'USD))
;; ⇒ #t

(money< (money :amount 1234 :currency 'USD)
        (money :amount 1500 :currency 'EUR))
;; ⇒ (result/error currency-mismatch
;;      (:a-currency USD :b-currency EUR :hint \"call money-convert first\"))
```

The error is deliberate. Silent conversion at compare time is a footgun.

## Time — always by instant

A time value carries its kind (`'instant`, `'calendar-date`) as a tag. `time<` compares only when both are the same kind; mixed kinds return a Result-error.

```scheme
(define (t/instant secs) (list 'instant secs))
(define (t/calendar-date s) (list 'calendar-date s))
(define (time-kind t) (car t))
(define (time< a b)
  (if (eq? (time-kind a) (time-kind b))
      (if (eq? (time-kind a) 'instant)
          (< (cadr a) (cadr b))
          (string<? (cadr a) (cadr b)))
      (result/error 'time-kind-mismatch)))
```

Two calendar dates in the same timezone compare by day. Two local date-times in the same timezone compare by wall clock. Two instants compare by seconds since epoch. Mixing kinds requires you to convert first.

```scheme
(time< (t/instant 1720000000) (t/instant 1720001000))
;; ⇒ #t

(time< (t/calendar-date \"2026-07-17\") (t/instant 1720000000))
;; ⇒ (result/error time-kind-mismatch ())
```

## Distance — same unit, then value

```scheme
(define (distance . fields) (cons 'distance fields))
(define (distance-unit d) (cadr d))    ;; first keyword names the unit
(define (distance-value d) (caddr d))
(define (distance< a b)
  (if (eq? (distance-unit a) (distance-unit b))
      (< (distance-value a) (distance-value b))
      (result/error 'unit-mismatch
        :ctx (list :a-unit (distance-unit a) :b-unit (distance-unit b)))))

(distance< (distance :meters 1500) (distance :meters 2000))
;; ⇒ #t

(distance< (distance :meters 1500) (distance :feet 5000))
;; ⇒ (result/error unit-mismatch ((:a-unit :meters :b-unit :feet)))
```

Convert first, then compare.

```scheme
(define (distance/to-meters d)
  (distance :meters (round (* (distance-value d) 3048/10000))))

(distance< (distance :meters 1500)
           (distance/to-meters (distance :feet 5000)))
;; ⇒ #t  (5000 feet ≈ 1524 meters, which is more than 1500)
```

## Percentages — never compare values without bases

```scheme
(define (percent value of base) (list 'percent :value value :base base))
(define (percent-value p) (caddr p))
(define (percent-base p) (list-ref p 4))
(define (percent-product p) (* (percent-value p) (percent-base p)))
(define (percent< a b) (< (percent-product a) (percent-product b)))

(percent< (percent 70 :of 10) (percent 75 :of 100))
;; ⇒ #t  (70% of 10 is smaller than 75% of 100)
```

Note this compares the *products*, not the percentage values. `percent-value<` (compare values) is a different verb that ignores bases:

```scheme
(define (percent-value< a b) (< (percent-value a) (percent-value b)))

(percent-value< (percent 70 :of 10) (percent 75 :of 100))
;; ⇒ #t (70 < 75, comparing values only)
```

Both exist because both are meaningful, and picking one silently would be wrong.

## Strings — lexicographic vs semantic

```scheme
;; string< is the standard R7RS string<? under a shorter local name;
;; natural-string< pulls the trailing digit runs out and compares them numerically.
(define (string< a b) (string<? a b))
(define (digits-tail s)
  (let loop ((i (- (string-length s) 1)) (acc '()))
    (if (and (>= i 0) (char-numeric? (string-ref s i)))
        (loop (- i 1) (cons (string-ref s i) acc))
        (if (null? acc) #f (string->number (list->string acc))))))
(define (natural-string< a b)
  (let ((na (digits-tail a)) (nb (digits-tail b)))
    (if (and na nb) (< na nb) (string<? a b))))

(string< \"abc\" \"abd\")
;; ⇒ #t (lexicographic)

(string< \"file2\" \"file10\")
;; ⇒ #f (lexicographic — \"1\" comes before \"2\" character-by-character)

(natural-string< \"file2\" \"file10\")
;; ⇒ #t (natural — 2 < 10)
```

Same strings, opposite answers. Which one you want is a design decision. Neither is universal.

## Sorting

The `sort` verb takes a comparison. Choose the right comparison for the kind.

```scheme
(define my-money-list
  (list (money :amount 1500 :currency 'USD)
        (money :amount 999  :currency 'USD)))
(define my-date-list (list (t/instant 1720001000) (t/instant 1720000000)))
(define my-filename-list (list \"file10\" \"file2\"))

(sort my-money-list money<)
(sort my-date-list time<)
(sort my-filename-list natural-string<)
```

If you use plain `<` on a list of typed values, you get an error at the first mixed comparison. The old habit — sort by whatever comes to hand — does not work when the values carry their kinds.

## Equality

Equality has its own family.

```scheme
(define (money-eq? a b)
  (and (eq? (field a :currency) (field b :currency))
       (= (field a :amount) (field b :amount))))
(define (money-value-eq? a b) (= (field a :amount) (field b :amount)))
(define (time-eq? a b)
  (and (eq? (time-kind a) (time-kind b)) (equal? (cadr a) (cadr b))))
(define (vector-eq? a b) (equal? a b))
(define (vector-approx? a b eps-key eps)
  (let loop ((i 0))
    (cond ((= i (vector-length a)) #t)
          ((> (abs (- (vector-ref a i) (vector-ref b i))) eps) #f)
          (else (loop (+ i 1))))))

(define a (money :amount 1234 :currency 'USD))
(define b (money :amount 1234 :currency 'USD))
(money-eq? a b)         ;; same currency AND same amount
(money-value-eq? a b)   ;; same amount regardless of currency
(vector-eq? (vector 1 2) (vector 1 2))        ;; element-wise
(vector-approx? (vector 1.0 2.0) (vector 1.0005 2.0) :epsilon 0.001)  ;; within a tolerance
```

Approximate equality with an explicit epsilon is often what floating-point comparisons need. Plain `=` on floats is a source of bugs; `approx?` with a named tolerance is honest.

## Ordering with mixed types

If you have a list containing multiple kinds — say, a heterogeneous timeline of events — you cannot use a single comparison verb. Either bucket by kind and sort each bucket, or convert to a common representation (canonical instants, in the timeline case) and sort by that.

```scheme
(define (event-instant e) (cadr e))   ;; pulls a canonical instant from any event
(define timeline (list (list 'event 1720001000) (list 'event 1720000000)))

(sort timeline
      (lambda (a b)
        (< (event-instant a) (event-instant b))))
```

The `event-instant` verb extracts an instant from any kind of event. Now the sort is over instants, which have a natural ordering.

## Try this

Grab a list of typed values from your codebase and try to sort it with plain `<`. Note the error. Rewrite with the kind-appropriate comparison. Note that the code is now longer but says what it means.

The comparison verbs feel bureaucratic on day one. By day thirty, they are the reason your reports do not have off-by-one bugs.")
